{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01DEdE5KQyDJaEibygN4mQQv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be built next.\n\n## Project State Analysis\n\n**What problem this project solves:**\ngo-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. The goal is to derive formal specifications from C source code, generate Go implementations using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n\n\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current state:**\nSpecs are complete for the core packages and utilities through various release versions, with generated code available at the generation-merged tags for the testutils and command implementations.\n\n**Current release:**\nThe roadmap shows we're actively working on rel01.0 for the ts command, with subsequent batches queued up. All specification work is done, but the working branch doesn't have any generated code yet—it's all sitting at those generation-merged tags.\n\nFor rel01.0-uc001-ts, I need to generate two pieces: the differential testing harness in pkg/testutils and the ts command implementation itself. Given the constraint of creating just one task, I'm weighing which makes the most sense to tackle first. Both are marked as not started, but the testutils would be a logical foundation since the ts implementation will likely depend on it for testing.\n\nLooking at the testutils PRD, it covers the core data structures, binary execution, comparison logic, and file-state handling—all foundational pieces. The guidelines suggest splitting implementation and tests separately, so my first task should focus on the types and interfaces that define the testing harness. Looking at the prd001-testutils requirements, I need to structure this across three files following the naming convention: the DiffTest struct goes in difftest.go, the RunDiffTests harness in harness.go, and the TimestampNormalizer in normalizer.go. The five requirement groups (R1 through R5) cover the struct definition, binary execution, comparison logic, normalization hooks, and file-state comparison—all of which fit together as one cohesive task since they're interdependent.\n\nGiven the 150-250 line budget and the planning principle that small types can combine with implementation, I should merge the type definitions (R1 and R4) directly into the implementation files rather than splitting them out separately. This keeps everything within scope while maintaining the required file structure. I'm weighing whether to prioritize the implementation or tests for this task. Since the implementation needs to come first according to the deterministic ordering principle, I'll focus task 0 on building out the pkg/testutils package with the DiffTest struct, harness, and TimestampNormalizer—covering all five requirements. The tests can follow as a separate task once the core functionality is in place. I'm laying out the core requirements for the testutils package: defining the DiffTest struct with fields for command arguments, stdin, environment variables, expected exit codes, normalizers, and file expectations; establishing the Normalize function type as a simple byte-slice transformer; and implementing RunDiffTests to execute each test case as a named subtest that compares binary outputs.\n\nNow I need to handle the execution details—running both binaries with matching parameters, capturing their output streams and exit codes, and applying a configurable timeout before comparing the normalized results byte-for-byte, reporting any divergences with full context including truncated stdin and both binaries' outputs.\n\nThe TimestampNormalizer will strip out strftime-formatted timestamps matching the default pattern and replace them with a placeholder, and I'll also support file-state validation through FileExpectation to check that output files match their expected content after execution.\n\nFor the implementation structure, DiffTest is the primary exported type living in difftest.go, and I'm using context.WithTimeout to enforce the per-binary timeout constraints. The DiffTest struct needs to include ExitCode, Normalizers, and FileExpectations fields alongside Args, Stdin, and Env. RunDiffTests should execute both binaries with the same inputs, compare their stdout, stderr, and exit codes, and report mismatches with the relevant args and output snippets. I'm also implementing a TimestampNormalizer that replaces timestamp patterns with a placeholder so tests can pass regardless of wall-clock differences, plus logic to read and byte-compare files when FileExpectations is set, and ensuring the linter passes without warnings.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the requirements more carefully—R1.1 specifies a single \"optional normalization function\" but R4.3 requires accepting a slice of Normalize functions applied in order, so I need to resolve whether the struct should hold one function or multiple.\n\nLooking at the acceptance criteria, I also need to determine if DiffTest should have a Name field for R3.6's requirement that individual test failures be identifiable through t.Run subtests, even though the PRD doesn't explicitly define this field. Let me check what AC1 through AC3 actually require about the test structure and the TimestampNormalizer behavior. these are supporting files that house RunDiffTests and TimestampNormalizer respectively. I'm thinking about whether to combine the type definitions with the implementation—the structs and interfaces are small enough that merging steps 1 and 2 makes sense. Looking at my line estimates, I'm projecting around 160 lines total across the three files, which fits comfortably within a single task.\n\nLet me verify the module name from go.mod and check the requirements again. I need to add a Name field to DiffTest for named subtests as R3.6 requires, and I should include a Dir field to support custom working directories per R2.5. With these design decisions locked in, I'm ready to write the final task description. I need to add a normalizer module for timestamp handling, and then define the Normalize function type and FileExpectation struct to support the core differential testing infrastructure. and R5.1.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that runs each DiffTest as t.Run(test.Name, ...) per prd001-testutils R3.6.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"For each DiffTest, invoke both goBinary and refBinary with identical Args, Stdin, Env, and Dir; capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each; enforce a 10-second timeout per invocation via context.WithTimeout; fail with a clear timeout message if exceeded per prd001-testutils R2.1-R2.5.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Apply DiffTest.Normalizers slice in order to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison; when the slice is nil or empty, apply no normalization per prd001-testutils R4.1 and R4.3.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte, normalized stderr byte-for-byte, and exit codes exactly; on any mismatch report with: Args, Stdin truncated to 256 bytes, ref stdout, Go stdout, ref stderr, Go stderr, and both exit codes per prd001-testutils R3.1-R3.5.\"\n      - id: R7\n        text: \"Implement TimestampNormalizer (var TimestampNormalizer Normalize) that replaces all substrings matching the ts default timestamp format '%b %d %H:%M:%S' (e.g., 'Feb 19 12:34:56') with the fixed placeholder 'TIMESTAMP' per prd\"\n\nNow I'm defining the TimestampNormalizer to handle the standard timestamp format by replacing matches with a fixed placeholder. I also need to handle file comparisons after both binaries finish—reading each expected file from the working directory and comparing its content byte-for-byte, reporting any differences in the same format as stdout mismatches. For the working directory setup, when DiffTest.Dir is empty I'll create an isolated temporary directory per test using t.TempDir() and pass it to both binary invocations.\n\nI'm also setting up timeout enforcement using exec.CommandContext with a 10-second timeout derived from context.WithTimeout, reporting any timeout failures via t.Errorf. The design keeps the primary source file as difftest.go since DiffTest is the main exported type, and I'll ensure no output is produced on passing tests—only calling t.Errorf when something actually fails. Let me verify the scope against the planning guidelines—I have 8 requirements which hits the upper bound, 6 acceptance criteria matching the PRD, and 4 design decisions all within the 3-5 range. The file count is at 3 when the guideline suggests 5-7, but for a focused pkg/testutils implementation, this feels appropriately scoped.\n\nI don't need to modify go.mod since the module already exists, and I should skip adding a package doc file to stay compliant with P7. The task description pulls in the right references—the PRD spec, architecture context, and module info—so the stitch agent should have everything needed to implement this.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the field name for normalization functions in DiffTest. R4.3 specifies a slice of Normalize functions, so I should use `Normalizers []Normalize` to match that requirement. For the TimestampNormalizer, I need to define a regex pattern that captures common strftime formats like \"Feb 19 12:34:56\"...\n\nI don't need to hardcode the regex in the task itself—the implementation should derive it from the PRD requirements. I should also add a `Name` field to DiffTest since R3.6 requires running each test as a named subtest, and having explicit names makes the output more readable. Test case names like \"default_format_three_lines\" would map naturally to this field.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73348,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73348},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f0384ad-ea3c-41e7-9bf8-6264d01bcd45","uuid":"4bdf5f23-bcf1-4d02-aeb9-21878c30affa"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01DEdE5KQyDJaEibygN4mQQv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils generates Go implementations of classic Unix utilities from formal specs derived from C source, verified by differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `pkg/testutils/` — differential testing harness (runs Go binary vs. reference binary, compares outputs)\n- `pkg/sys/` — Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions (terminal width, extended stat, signals)\n- `pkg/format/` — shared output formatting (column alignment, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `cmd/<utility>/` — one Go binary per utility, with differential tests alongside\n- `magefiles/` — mage build targets; boundary between human-guided spec phase and automated synthesis\n\n**Current state:** All specs are complete. Generated code lives at `generation-merged` tags but no code exists on the working branch. The working branch has specs only.\n\n**Current release:** rel01.0 (Batch 1 — ts). The use case `rel01.0-uc001-ts` is in_progress, codestatus: not started. This requires `pkg/testutils` (the harness) before cmd/ts tests can run. Per P8 (libraries before consumers), `pkg/testutils` implementation comes first.\n\n---\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001-testutils R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component section)\n      - go.mod (module name: github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"DiffTest struct, Normalize function type, FileExpectation struct\"\n      - path: pkg/testutils/harness.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"RunDiffTests -- binary execution, subtest dispatch, comparison, and divergence reporting\"\n      - path: pkg/testutils/normalizer.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"TimestampNormalizer built-in normalizer\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Define DiffTest struct with fields: Name (string), Args ([]string), Stdin ([]byte),\n          Env ([]string), ExitCode (int), Normalizers ([]Normalize), Dir (string), and\n          FileExpectations ([]FileExpectation) per prd001-testutils R1.1-R1.4 and R5.1.\n          Nil Stdin means the binary receives no stdin (EOF immediately); nil Env means\n          inherit the test process environment with no overrides.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Define Normalize function type as func([]byte) []byte per prd001-testutils R1.4.\n          Define FileExpectation struct with Path (string) and Content ([]byte) fields\n          per prd001-testutils R5.1.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest)\n          that runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run(test.Name, ...) so individual\n          test case failures are identifiable in go test output per prd001-testutils R3.6.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"For each DiffTest, invoke both goBinary and refBinary with identical Args, Stdin,\n          Env, and Dir; capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each independently;\n          enforce a 10-second timeout per invocation; fail the subtest with a clear timeout\n          message if either binary exceeds the timeout per prd001-testutils R2.1-R2.5.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Apply DiffTest.Normalizers slice in order to both binaries' stdout and stderr before\n          comparison; when the slice is nil or empty apply no normalization per\n          prd001-testutils R4.1 and R4.3.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte, normalized stderr byte-for-byte, and exit\n          codes exactly; on any mismatch call t.Errorf with: Args, Stdin truncated to 256\n          bytes, reference stdout, Go stdout, reference stderr, Go stderr, and both exit\n          codes; produce no output on passing tests per prd001-testutils R3.1-R3.5.\"\n      - id: R7\n        text: \"Implement TimestampNormalizer as a package-level variable of type Normalize that\n          replaces all substrings matching the ts default timestamp pattern '%b %d %H:%M:%S'\n          (e.g., 'Feb 19 12:34:56') with the fixed placeholder string 'TIMESTAMP' using\n          a compiled regexp per prd001-testutils R4.2.\"\n      - id: R8\n        text: \"After both binaries exit, for each FileExpectation read the file at Path (resolved\n          relative to the binary's working directory) and compare its content to Content\n          byte-for-byte; report divergence using the same t.Errorf format as stdout\n          divergence per prd001-testutils R5.1-R5.2.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"The file difftest.go is the primary source file because DiffTest is the primary\n          exported type; testutils/testutils.go is forbidden per planning constitution P7\n          (package primary source file named after primary exported type, not the package name).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Working directory: when DiffTest.Dir is empty, create a per-test temporary directory\n          via t.TempDir() and pass the same directory to both binary invocations so each test\n          is isolated per prd001-testutils R2.5.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Timeout enforcement: use exec.CommandContext with a context derived from\n          context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 10*time.Second) for each binary\n          invocation; detect context.DeadlineExceeded and report via t.Errorf with the\n          phrase 'timed out' per prd001-testutils R2.3.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Divergence reporting: t.Errorf (not t.Fatalf) so all subtests in the batch run\n          even when one fails; no output is produced on passing tests per prd001-testutils AC2.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/testutils/... succeeds with no errors.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"DiffTest struct has all required fields: Name, Args, Stdin, Env, ExitCode,\n          Normalizers, Dir, FileExpectations.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"RunDiffTests signature is RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string,\n          tests []DiffTest); a cmd/ package can call it from a TestXxx function with only a\n          []DiffTest slice and two binary paths -- no other setup required per prd001-testutils AC1.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer applied to the byte slice 'Feb 19 12:34:56 hello' returns\n          'TIMESTAMP hello' per prd001-testutils AC3.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"A DiffTest with a non-empty FileExpectations entry causes RunDiffTests to read\n          the named file after execution and compare its content; a content mismatch produces\n          a t.Errorf failure per prd001-testutils AC4.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"golangci-lint ./pkg/testutils/... reports no warnings.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73348,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73348},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f0384ad-ea3c-41e7-9bf8-6264d01bcd45","uuid":"a31ab0b8-45e5-4dfc-9b0d-e18de2308394"}
